-- title:
--   Coordinatewise bound by the Euclidean norm
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$, where $\mathbb{R}^d$ carries the Euclidean ($\ell^2$) norm $\lVert x \rVert = \bigl(\sum_{j} x_j^2\bigr)^{1/2}$. Then for every index $i$,
--
--   $$|x_i| \le \lVert x \rVert .$$
--
--   In other words the maximum norm is dominated by the Euclidean norm. This is the elementary half of the comparison between the two norms on $\mathbb{R}^d$ (the other half, $\lVert x\rVert \le \sqrt{d}\,\lVert x\rVert_\infty$, is what converts cube membership into ball membership); it is used throughout to pass from coordinatewise information about a point of a cube to metric information.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement is for `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`, so $x_i$ is the $i$-th coordinate of $x$ under the canonical identification with functions $\mathrm{Fin}\,d \to \mathbb{R}$; no hypothesis on $d$ is needed, the case $d = 0$ being vacuous since there are no indices.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Cubes.lean#L38-L44

